    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for LawDog centers on two marquee bats who anchor the lineup with high-efficiency output: **Lawrence Butler** (73.95) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (71.86). **Lawrence Butler** is our standout play against Kumar Rocker; the combination of a 10% park boost and a significant platoon edge (+13%) makes him a high-upside start for power and potential run production. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a must-start despite the matchup against Michael King; his ability to drive the ball against a 4.40 xERA arm, combined with an elite +6% adjustment for his top-of-the-order role, secures his spot even with the slight penalty for King\u2019s ability to hold runners.\n\nMid-tier reliability is provided by **Gleyber Torres** (59.88) and **Zack Gelof** (59.83), both of whom benefit from favorable lineup placement and platoon alignments. **Gleyber Torres** in particular has seen a massive surge in his score due to an expected +13% platoon boost against the lefty Sean Newcomb, making him a cornerstone for today\u2019s points. Conversely, the most difficult decision on the bench involved **Jeremiah Jackson** (48.65). While he offers a unique advantage due to the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery\u2014bolstering his stolen base potential\u2014his lower batting order position (-6%) ultimately relegated him to the bench behind higher-projected options. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board, as these scores assume standardized batting positions; any unexpected drop for **Yainer Diaz** (46.19) or **JJ Bleday** (50.07) could further compress their marginal scores and potentially push them below our efficiency floor. Additionally, the wind-in factor affecting **Masyn Winn** creates a slight drag on his upside, and while **Dylan Beavers** benefits from the stolen base environment against Steven Matz, he faces a stiff platoon disadvantage (-9%) that keeps him firmly in the middle of our projections. As 